[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known: one issue, one fix queued**. Read report by report,
it holds three problems with three different fixes. The queued parser fix closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships. The other two problems have their own tickets now;
their reporters are still waiting.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{
  parser: 'https://github.com/sampeppard/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1',
  hint: 'https://github.com/sampeppard/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2',
  shift: 'https://github.com/sampeppard/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3',
}} />

Every number above is computed from `reports-4471.json` on build, so it stays true as the desk
keeps merging. Reports are counted once per id: the export pages overlap.

## How the reports were split

1. **By tenant, not by intake tag.** A report's cause depends on where the tenant is and which
   booking form they run (`causes.ts`). The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser.
2. **`hint`** is any day-first tenant's report that came in through the booking-v3 form, which
   prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says; the form reads it day first.
   Fixing the parser does not fix the hint.
3. **`shift`** is any report whose body describes a move by one day — 12 May shown as 11 May,
   Tuesday shown as Monday, Lisbon and Toronto seeing different days. A day/month swap can never do
   that, so these are not the parser bug, whatever tenant sent them. The desk had already noted
   them (`desk-notes.json`) but counted them under `parser`; `problems.ts` reads the body and
   keeps them apart.

## Why it was one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. "Appointment moved
by itself" fits all three problems, so all three landed in one thread. Its stated consequence —
different subjects, same bug, kept apart — happened the other way round here: same subjects,
different bugs, merged.

